14.steps:
1.Login to the FW from the default LAN interface;
  2.Go to Users>Setting, choose the LDAP/RADIUS or "LDAP + Local User"/"RADIUS + Local Users" for authentication method, and apply;
  3. From the PC1, enter "http://x0 interface ip of the FW", make sure there is page prompting "Please be patient as you are being re-directed to a secure login page", and then the page will turn to HTTPS login page; 4. Choose the "Local Users" as the authentication method and apply;
  5. Go to Network>Interfaces, make sure the HTTP user login of each interfaces turn to be active.

33.steps:
1.Login to the FW as admin from the default WAN interface;
  2.Go to the Network>Interfaces, open the config dialog of the interface X2 just assigned to LAN zone;
  3.Modify the IP Assignment from Static to L2 Bridged Mode, and choose WAN interface X1 from the "Bridged to" drop-down box;
  4.Verify the LAN interface IP turn to be as same as the WAN interface IP, and the IP Assignment is "Secondary Bridged I/F", while the IP Assignment of X1 is "Primary Bridged I/F";
  5.Change the PC2's IP into the WAN subnet, and make sure from PC2 user can manage FW and access resource on WAN side;
  6.Restart the FW;
  7.Verify the last configuration is intact after reboot;
  8.Repeat the steps by bridging to secondary WAN interface and another LAN interface;

38.steps:
1.Login to the FW as admin from the default LAN interface;
  2). Navigate to Network>Interfaces page, on the default WAN interface edit dialog, configure default WAN to DHCP mode and apply;
  3). After the WAN interface get DHCP IP address, access the WAN interface from a PC in the same network, make sure it is successful;

67.steps:
1). Login to the FW;
2. Configure the two WAN interfaces(x2,x3) to L2TP/PPPoE/PPTP and connect success;
3. Go to Networking->Failover&LB, set x3 as default wan;
4. Change the WAN interface x2 to unassigned.
